ABOUT=heading('About','Curiosity is <span class="gradient-text">my common thread.</span>','A mathematician with a background in physics, an interest in computation, and a belief that abstract ideas and practical problems make each other more interesting.')+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell two-col"><div class="body-copy"><p class="lead">I am a PhD student in mathematics at North Carolina State University, based in Raleigh.</p><p>My undergraduate training was in both mathematics and physics at Northern Arizona University, where I later earned an M.S. in mathematics. Those experiences shaped the way I approach research: I like formal structure, but I also like seeing mathematical ideas tested against models, data, and computation.</p><p>My master's work used topological data analysis and machine learning to recover parameters from patterns generated by a partial differential equation. In another project I studied chaos in a double pendulum. I have also collaborated on published research in the algebraic theory of impartial combinatorial games.</p><p>Alongside research, I have spent several years teaching mathematics, including helping establish a Putnam preparation program.</p><p>I am especially interested in opportunities involving scientific machine learning, computational mathematics, numerical methods, and mathematical research.</p><div class="cta-row"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:{EMAIL}">Contact me {icon('arrow')}</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="assets/docs/resume.pdf">Industry resume {icon('down')}</a></div></div><div><div class="content-card"><p class="kicker">Education</p><div class="timeline"><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2026–present</span><h3>PhD · Mathematics</h3><p>North Carolina State University</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2023</span><h3>MS · Mathematics</h3><p>Northern Arizona University</p></div><div class="timeline-item"><span class="when">2021</span><h3>BS · Mathematics and BS · Physics</h3><p>Northern Arizona University</p></div></div></div><div class="content-card" style="margin-top:18px"><p class="kicker">Methods & tools</p><div class="skill-cloud">{''.join('<span class="tag">'+x+'</span>' for x in ['Python','NumPy / SciPy','scikit-learn','PyTorch','TensorFlow','MATLAB','Dynamical systems','PDEs','Persistent homology','GUDHI','Bayesian inference','Mathematical proof'])}</div></div></div></div></section>'''

TDA=heading('Case study / scientific machine learning','Can topology reveal <span class="gradient-text">hidden parameters?</span>','Master’s thesis · Northern Arizona University · 2023',True)+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell detail-layout"><article class="body-copy"><p class="lead">This project investigated whether topological features of simulated spatial patterns could help infer the parameter values that generated them.</p><h2>The problem</h2><p>Numerical solutions of a nonlinear partial differential equation can produce complex surfaces. Recovering model parameters from those snapshots is an inverse problem: observed patterns may be similar even when their underlying parameters differ.</p><p>I studied surfaces generated by an anisotropic Kuramoto–Sivashinsky-type PDE and investigated whether persistent homology could summarize their geometry in a form useful to machine-learning models.</p><h2>The computational workflow</h2><p>The study used <strong>850 surfaces of size 200 × 200</strong> across <strong>17 parameter classes</strong>, with 50 randomized initial conditions per class. I computed cubical persistent homology, transformed persistence information into engineered representations such as persistence images, and compared multiple classifiers.</p><p>The pipeline included cross-validation, hyperparameter searches, and comparisons between raw surfaces and topological features. Experiments used Python libraries and NAU's Monsoon computing resources.</p><h2>Selected results</h2><div class="content-card"><div class="metrics" style="margin:0;border:0;padding:0"><div class="metric"><b>79.96%</b><span>mean five-fold CV accuracy, RF + persistence images</span></div><div class="metric"><b>77.76%</b><span>best reported kNN with topological features</span></div><div class="metric"><b>10.71%</b><span>kNN using raw surfaces, comparison</span></div></div></div><p>These results are specific to the thesis dataset and its evaluation setup; they are not claims of performance on unseen physical systems.</p><h2>What I took away</h2><p>Representations matter. In this setting, persistent homology captured information that made classification substantially more effective for some models. The work brought together numerical PDE data, computational topology, and careful empirical evaluation.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/mishbolt/TDA-Thesis" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the code on GitHub ↗</a></p></article><aside class="aside-panel"><h2>At a glance</h2><dl><dt>Role</dt><dd>Master's thesis researcher</dd><dt>Institution</dt><dd>Northern Arizona University</dd><dt>Advisor</dt><dd>Rachel Neville</dd><dt>Year</dt><dd>2023</dd><dt>Techniques</dt><dd>Cubical persistence, persistence images, random forests, kNN, SVM, cross-validation</dd><dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/mishbolt/TDA-Thesis">mishbolt/TDA-Thesis ↗</a></dd></dl><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="../assets/docs/cv.pdf">Download full CV ↓</a></aside></div></section>'''

PENDULUM=heading('Case study / nonlinear dynamics','Finding structure <span class="gradient-text">inside chaos.</span>','Undergraduate research project · Northern Arizona University · 2020–2021',True)+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell detail-layout"><article class="body-copy"><p class="lead">The double pendulum offers an unusually rich laboratory for studying the boundary between regular and chaotic motion.</p><h2>From mechanics to equations</h2><p>I derived the system's equations using Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics, nondimensionalized the dynamics, and studied normal modes in the small-angle regime. Analytical approximations were checked against numerical solutions of the full nonlinear equations in MATLAB.</p><h2>Visualizing invisible phase space</h2><p>Because the phase space is four-dimensional, Poincaré sections provide a more accessible picture of motion. By recording intersections with a lower-dimensional section, one can investigate regular orbits, resonances, and regions showing chaotic behavior.</p><figure class="figure"><img src="../assets/images/poincare-sections.webp" alt="Two Poincaré section plots from the author's original double-pendulum report, with closed and broken curves" width="782" height="351" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Selected Poincaré sections from my double-pendulum research report, illustrating different dynamical regimes.</figcaption></figure><h2>Measuring sensitivity</h2><p>I investigated Lyapunov spectra by integrating variational equations and repeatedly orthonormalizing tangent vectors. I also explored Poincaré–Lindstedt perturbation analysis and numerical methods for periodic orbits.</p><p>This was an early project that helped connect my physics training to applied mathematics and scientific computing.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/mishbolt/MATLAB-Double-Pendulum" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MATLAB implementations on GitHub ↗</a></p></article><aside class="aside-panel"><h2>At a glance</h2><dl><dt>Role</dt><dd>Undergraduate researcher</dd><dt>Institution</dt><dd>Northern Arizona University</dd><dt>Support</dt><dd>Hooper Undergraduate Research Award</dd><dt>Time</dt><dd>2020–2021</dd><dt>Methods</dt><dd>Hamiltonian dynamics, numerical integration, perturbation theory, Lyapunov spectra</dd><dt>Code</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/mishbolt/MATLAB-Double-Pendulum">MATLAB-Double-Pendulum ↗</a></dd></dl><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="../projects.html">Other projects ↗</a></aside></div></section>'''

GAMES=heading('Case study / algebraic game theory','When games have <span class="gradient-text">algebraic structure.</span>','Peer-reviewed publication · Discrete Mathematics Letters · 2025',True)+f'''<section class="section-tight"><div class="shell detail-layout"><article class="body-copy"><p class="lead">Our research asks how the structural ideas behind homomorphisms, quotients, and isomorphism theorems extend to impartial combinatorial games.</p><h2>The idea</h2><p>Combinatorial games can be represented by directed graphs of positions and legal moves. The paper introduces <strong>optiongraphs</strong> and <strong>option-preserving maps</strong> as a framework broad enough to include games with cycles and infinite play.</p><h2>The mathematical result</h2><p>With Dana C. Ernst and Nándor Sieben, I coauthored a paper demonstrating that the <strong>four isomorphism theorems from universal algebra</strong> hold in this category. We studied congruence relations, quotient optiongraphs, and a minimum quotient construction.</p><p>The framework preserves important game-theoretic information such as outcomes, remoteness, and extended nim-values under suitable maps.</p><h2>Why it matters</h2><p>Abstract algebraic constructions can identify which distinctions between positions matter and which can be compressed away. This can produce smaller game models without losing the relevant information.</p><p><a href="{DOI}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the published article ↗</a></p></article><aside class="aside-panel"><h2>Publication</h2><dl><dt>Article</dt><dd>Isomorphism theorems for impartial combinatorial games</dd><dt>Authors</dt><dd>M. Baltushkin, D. C. Ernst, N. Sieben</dd><dt>Journal</dt><dd>Discrete Mathematics Letters 16 (2025), 59–66</dd><dt>DOI</dt><dd><a href="{DOI}">10.47443/dml.2025.071 ↗</a></dd></dl><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://www.dmlett.com/archive/v16/DML25_v16_pp59-66.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read PDF ↗</a></aside></div></section>'''

